concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-sexual-offences-against-children-act-2010-sch-1-sec-471-6 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "471 21  Defences in respect of child abuse material"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-1-sec-471-6 ;
    values:originalText "(1) A person is not criminally responsible for an offence against section 471.19 (using a postal or similar service for child abuse material) or 471.20 (possessing etc. child abuse material for use through a postal or similar service) because of engaging in particular conduct if the conduct:  (a) is of public benefit; and  (b) does not extend beyond what is of public benefit. In determining whether the person is, under this subsection, not criminally responsible for the offence, the question whether the conduct is of public benefit is a question of fact and the person’s motives in engaging in the conduct are irrelevant. Note: A defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in this subsection, see subsection 13.3(3).  (2) For the purposes of subsection (1), conduct is of public benefit if, and only if, the conduct is necessary for or of assistance in:  (a) enforcing a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory; or ComLaw Authoritative Act C2010A00042  Amendments relating to sexual offences against children  Schedule 1 Amendments relating to child sex offences involving postal or similar services, or carriage services  Part 2  (b) monitoring compliance with, or investigating a contravention of, a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory; or  (c) the administration of justice; or  (d) conducting scientific, medical or educational research that has been approved by the Minister in writing for the purposes of this section.  (3) A person is not criminally responsible for an offence against section 471.19 (using a postal or similar service for child abuse material) or 471.20 (possessing etc. child abuse material for use through a postal or similar service) if:  (a) the person is, at the time of the offence, a law enforcement officer, or an intelligence or security officer, acting in the course of his or her duties; and  (b) the conduct of the person is reasonable in the circumstances for the purpose of performing that duty. Note: A defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in this subsection, see subsection 13.3(3)." ;
    skos:definition "(1) A person is not criminally responsible for an offence against section 471.19 (using a postal or similar service for child abuse material) or 471.20 (possessing etc. child abuse material for use through a postal or similar service) because of engaging in particular conduct if the conduct:  (a) is of public benefit; and  (b) does not extend beyond what is of public benefit. In determining whether the person is, under this subsection, not criminally responsible for the offence, the question whether the conduct is of public benefit is a question of fact and the person’s motives in engaging in the conduct are irrelevant. Note: A defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in this subsection, see subsection 13.3(3).  (2) For the purposes of subsection (1), conduct is of public benefit if, and only if, the conduct is necessary for or of assistance in:  (a) enforcing a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory; or ComLaw Authoritative Act C2010A00042  Amendments relating to sexual offences against children  Schedule 1 Amendments relating to child sex offences involving postal or similar services, or carriage services  Part 2  (b) monitoring compliance with, or investigating a contravention of, a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory; or  (c) the administration of justice; or  (d) conducting scientific, medical or educational research that has been approved by the Minister in writing for the purposes of this section.  (3) A person is not criminally responsible for an offence against section 471.19 (using a postal or similar service for child abuse material) or 471.20 (possessing etc. child abuse material for use through a postal or similar service) if:  (a) the person is, at the time of the offence, a law enforcement officer, or an intelligence or security officer, acting in the course of his or her duties; and  (b) the conduct of the person is reasonable in the circumstances for the purpose of performing that duty. Note: A defendant be"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "27d5c717fe9dbecd05e1d8a189214651dd78d378e45625c3587979026aa1f08f" ;
    cof:pageNumber "42"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-sexual-offences-against-children-act-2010-sch-1-sec-471-6-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-sexual-offences-against-children-act-2010-sch-1-sec-471-6-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-sexual-offences-against-children-act-2010-sch-1-sec-471-6-ss-3 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <crimes-legislation-amendment-sexual-offences-against-children-act-2010.pdf> ;
    dc:source "crimes-legislation-amendment-sexual-offences-against-children-act-2010.pdf, p.42" .
